This file contains information on how to build Marinetti.

Prerequisites
-------------

To build Marinetti, you will need an installed and functioning copy
of Merlin 16+ Z08 for GS/OS. Merlin 16+ Z08 is Richard's unofficial
custom version of Merlin 16+ 4.12, which is available from:

http://macgui.com/downloads/?file_id=25486

This archive only contains the Z08 changes, so you will first need to
install the full Merlin 16+ package. Merlin 16+ was published by Roger Wagner Publishing, which is available from:

http://macgui.com/downloads/?file_id=11104

Optionally, you may also like to obtain Richard's commands for Merlin.
Two commands in particular are very useful - SHOWRVERSION and SETRVERSION.
They are available from Richard's web site:

http://www.kashum.com/a2lib/m16.exe.bxy

You will also need to install the tool interface macros for System
Software 6.0.1. One source of these is Sheppy's site:
http://www.sheppyware.net/downloads/downloads-gs/files/Macs.Sys6.SHK
However, the Marinetti source files expect that the System 6.0.1 macros
have been added to the appropriate Merlin macro files. Sheppy's System.6.macs.s
file itself cannot be used as-is because of memory constraints during the build.
You can either split up and add the System 6 macros to every Merlin macro file,
or attempt to build and resolve any macro errors as you go.

From here on down, it is assumed that Merlin Z08 is installed and working.

Installing the tools
--------------------

All of the build tools are kept in the CVS repository. After you have
extracted everything from the archive, move the following files to their
final locations.

Copy the file Tools:Merlin:Command:COPYFORK into the COMMANDS folder of your
Merlin installation.

Copy the file Tools:Merlin:Help.Files:COPYFORK into the HELP.FILES folder of
your Merlin installation.

Copy the file Tools:Merlin:Macros:RJBUTIL.MACS.S to your Merlin macros folder.

Make sure Merlin has prefix 4 set to your macros folder. If using the PROJECT
command, you can change the prefix from the disk command line and save the
project back without having to reassemble your Merlin PARMS.S file.

Your environment is now ready to build Marinetti.


Working with the source files
-----------------------------

The source files are available in the MOSP CVS repository.
Refer to the PrepareDevEnv document for details on how to obtain them.

Once the CVS repository has been checked out, in order to work with a file
properly within Merlin you'll need to load it into the Merlin editor, and
convert it to Merlin source format.
To do this, load the file, press OA-O to get the command box, and type
"FIXS" (without the quotes). Now save the source by pressing OA-Q to
get back to the main menu, and press "S" then "Y" (both without the quotes)
to save.

Source files are kept within the CVS repository as straight ASCII SRC files,
so that CVS diff and browsing utilities may be used.


Building the Marinetti Init
---------------------------

The Marinetti Init has a complex build process that involves assembling
source files, linking to object files where the source is not yet released,
creating a resource fork and copying the result to the installation
location. This process is performed automatically by Merlin under the
control of a link file.

If you have followed the PrepareDevEnv document successfully then your development environment contains the folder Marinetti:Init that holds all
the link files, the resource file, and all the currently open sourced source
code.

To build the Marinetti Init:

1. Launch Merlin 16+, prefix to the folder containing the Marinetti code,
 and enter the editor. (Press "D" and type "prefix xxx" to move around)

2. Once in the editor, press OA-O to get the command box.

3. Type: link'i.all

4. If everything seems OK, you have just assembled the included source,
 linked it with the other modules, generated the permanent init (PIF),
 copied the resources into it, and copied the entire thing into your
 *:System:System.Setup folder.

5. To just assemble the files that have changed, type: link'i.mods
 To do just the link (no assembly), type: link'i

Other components of Marinetti have a similar process. The HowToRelease
document has a list of the components and their build files.

